WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.530
Now we are ready to put the genome

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:07.100
assembly problem into the language of
computer scientists.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.980
Let's try to formalize this problem.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.460
So the genome sequencing problem: input is
a collection of string.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.919
Output is a string, Genome, constructed
from reads.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.240
Is it a well formalized problem?

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.300
Let's ask Pavel! Last time, remember
in the replication chapter, I

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.120
cricitized Pavel for not giving a well
defined problem.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.080
Now let's ask him.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.330
Well this is not a computational problem.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:48.570
Okay, so allow me to first define what is
k-mer composition of a string.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.480
Given a string, a k-mer composition of a
string

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.540
is a set of all k-mers in that string.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.730
So we can order the k-mers in this order,

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.900
according to the positions where they
appear in the genome.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.930
But please note that the position
information is not available.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:18.162
So a more correct way to order these
k-mers is

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:24.970
in a lexicographic order, like in the
dictionary.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:29.420
The string reconstruction problem:
Input: a collection of k-mers.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.192
Output: a genome such that the k-mer
composition of

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:36.683
the genome is equal to the collection of
k-mers.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.570
So we have a well-defined problem.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.670
And what next is to build intuitions
and algorithms to solve this problem.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.010
What is the most natural way to

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.610
solve this?
Well, let's start with TAA

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:58.670
and assume that this is the first 3-mer
of the genome.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:06.376
If TAA is the first k-mer of the genome,
then what is the second k-mer?

00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:09.300
The second k-mer should start with AA.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.670
This one k-mer starting with AA, that is
AAT.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.860
Then what is the next k-mer?
The next k-mer should start with AT.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.600
Here, we have three AT, but they are all
ATG, so we choose one.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.400
What is the next k-mer?
The next k-mer should start with TG.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.750
Here we have three k-mers starting with
TG.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.180
Which one would we choose?

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.210
I don't know.
Let's pick a random one.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.830
Okay, so the next k-mer should start with
GT.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.400
Here we have one k-mer starting with GT.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.540
And the next one TT.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.690
So the next k-mer should start with
TT.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.060
But in our collection there's no k-mer
starting with TT.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.870
Well then certainly there is a need

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:18.200
to develop new algorithms for solving this
problem.

